      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, I was just about to say the escrow agent is like an organized, slightly nervous little **goblin** who has to make sure no one steals the treasure (the house) or gets eaten by a dragon (a legal loophole).\n\nSo, to finish that thought: they are like a goblin who has to guard the gold while the humans argue over who gets the sword. They don't actually own the money; they just hold it in a special, locked box until the contract says it's okay to open. If you skip the goblin and give the gold directly to the seller, the seller might be a goblin who eats the gold and disappears, leaving you with nothing but an empty hand and a very angry goblin on your tail.\n\nThe goblin (escrow agent) ensures that the seller gets the deed *only after* you've paid the money, and you get the keys *only after* the seller has signed the papers. It's a little goblin dance where the music stops if anyone tries to cheat. Without the goblin, the whole transaction is just a chaotic goblin market where everyone loses their stuff.\n\nBasically, the goblin is there to keep the goblins from eating each",
